sn1.10:5.6 | P: Vaggo tena pavuccati. | T: (none)

## sn1.42
sn1.42:1.1 | S: “Giving what do you give strength? | T: A deva: “A giver of what is a giver of strength?
sn1.42:1.2 | S: Giving what do you give beauty? | T: A giver of what, a giver of beauty?
sn1.42:1.3 | S: Giving what do you give happiness? | T: A giver of what, a giver of ease?
sn1.42:1.4 | S: Giving what do you give vision? | T: A giver of what, a giver of vision?
sn1.42:1.5 | S: And who is the giver of all? | T: And who is a giver of everything?
sn1.42:1.6 | S: Please answer my question.” | T: Being asked, please explain this to me.”
sn1.42:2.1 | S: “Giving food you give strength. | T: The Buddha: “A giver of food is a giver of strength.
sn1.42:2.2 | S: Giving clothes you give beauty. | T: A giver of clothes, a giver of beauty.
sn1.42:2.3 | S: Giving a vehicle you give happiness. | T: A giver of a vehicle, a giver of ease.
sn1.42:2.4 | S: Giving a lamp you give vision. | T: A giver of a lamp, a giver of vision.
sn1.42:3.1 | S: And the giver of all | T: And the one who gives a residence is the one who’s a giver of everything.
sn1.42:3.2 | S: is the one who gives monastic quarters. | T: (none)
sn1.42:3.3 | S: But one who teaches the Dhamma | T: But the one who teaches the Dhamma
sn1.42:3.4 | S: gives the gift of freedom from death.” | T: is a giver of the Deathless.”

## sn10.4
sn10.4:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Magadhans at the Maṇimālika Shrine, the haunt of the native spirit Maṇibhadda. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Magadhans at the Jewel-stand Shrine, the haunt of the yakkha-spirit, Maṇibhadda [Auspicious Jewel].
sn10.4:1.2 | S: Then the native spirit Maṇibhadda went up to the Buddha, and spoke this verse in the Buddha’s presence: | T: Then Maṇibhadda the yakkha-spirit went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, recited this verse:
sn10.4:2.1 | S: “It’s always auspicious for the mindful; | T: “It’s always auspicious for one who is mindful.
sn10.4:2.2 | S: the mindful prosper in happiness. | T: The mindful one prospers happily—always.
sn10.4:2.3 | S: Each new day is better for the mindful, | T: The mindful one grows better each day
sn10.4:2.4 | S: and they are freed from enmity.” | T: and is totally freed from animosity.”
sn10.4:3.1 | S: “It’s always auspicious for the mindful; | T: The Buddha: “It’s always auspicious for one who is mindful.
sn10.4:3.2 | S: the mindful prosper in happiness. | T: The mindful one prospers happily always.
sn10.4:3.3 | S: Each new day is better for the mindful, | T: The mindful one grows better each day
sn10.4:3.4 | S: but they’re not freed from enmity. | T: but isn’t totally freed from animosity.
sn10.4:4.1 | S: But someone whose mind delights in harmlessness, | T: Whoever’s heart, all day, all night, delights in harmlessness
sn10.4:4.2 | S: all day and all night, | T: (none)
sn10.4:4.3 | S: with love for all living creatures— | T: with goodwill for all beings
sn10.4:4.4 | S: they have no enmity for anyone.” | T: has no animosity with anyone at all.

## sn48.39
sn48.39:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five faculties. | T: “Monks, there are these five faculties.
sn48.39:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
sn48.39:1.3 | S: The faculties of pleasure, pain, happiness, sadness, and equanimity. | T: The pleasure-faculty, the pain-faculty, the happiness-faculty, the distress-faculty, the equanimity-faculty.
sn48.39:1.4 | S: The faculty of pleasure arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as pleasant. | T: “In dependence on a contact to be experienced as pleasure, the pleasure-faculty arises.
sn48.39:1.5 | S: When in a state of pleasure, you understand: ‘I’m in a state of pleasure.’ | T: Being eased, one discerns, ‘I am eased.’
sn48.39:1.6 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as pleasant, you understand that the corresponding faculty of pleasure that had arisen ceases and stops. | T: With the cessation of that very contact to be experienced as pleasure, one discerns, ‘What was experienced as coming from that—the pleasure-faculty arising in dependence on a contact to be experienced as pleasure—ceases & grows still.’
sn48.39:2.1 | S: The faculty of pain arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as painful. | T: “In dependence on a contact to be experienced as pain, the pain-faculty arises.
sn48.39:2.2 | S: When in a state of pain, you understand: ‘I’m in a state of pain.’ | T: Being pained, one discerns, ‘I am pained.’
sn48.39:2.3 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as painful, you understand that the corresponding faculty of pain that had arisen ceases and stops. | T: With the cessation of that very contact to be experienced as pain, one discerns, ‘What was experienced as coming from that—the pain-faculty arising in dependence on a contact to be experienced as pain—ceases & grows still.’
sn48.39:3.1 | S: The faculty of happiness arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as happiness. | T: “In dependence on a contact to be experienced as happiness, the happiness-faculty arises.
sn48.39:3.2 | S: When in a state of happiness, you understand: ‘I’m in a state of happiness.’ | T: Being happy, one discerns, ‘I am happy.’
sn48.39:3.3 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as happiness, you understand that the corresponding faculty of happiness that had arisen ceases and stops. | T: With the cessation of that very contact to be experienced as happiness, one discerns, ‘What was experienced as coming from that—the happiness-faculty arising in dependence on a contact to be experienced as happiness—ceases & grows still.’
sn48.39:4.1 | S: The faculty of sadness arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as sadness. | T: “In dependence on a contact to be experienced as distress, the distress-faculty arises.
sn48.39:4.2 | S: When in a state of sadness, you understand: ‘I’m in a state of sadness.’ | T: Being distressed, one discerns, ‘I am distressed.’
sn48.39:4.3 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as sadness, you understand that the corresponding faculty of sadness that had arisen ceases and stops. | T: With the cessation of that very contact to be experienced as distress, one discerns, ‘What was experienced as coming from that—the distress-faculty arising in dependence on a contact to be experienced as distress—ceases & grows still.’
sn48.39:5.1 | S: The faculty of equanimity arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as equanimous. | T: “In dependence on a contact to be experienced as equanimity, the equanimity-faculty arises.
sn48.39:5.2 | S: When in a state of equanimity, you understand: ‘I’m in a state of equanimity.’ | T: Being equanimous, one discerns, ‘I am equanimous.’
sn48.39:5.3 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as equanimous, you understand that the corresponding faculty of equanimity that had arisen ceases and stops. | T: With the cessation of that very contact to be experienced as equanimity, one discerns, ‘What was experienced as coming from that—the equanimity-faculty arising in dependence on a contact to be experienced as equanimity—ceases & grows still.’
sn48.39:6.1 | S: When you rub two sticks together, heat is generated and fire is produced. But when you part the sticks and lay them aside, any corresponding heat ceases and stops. | T: “Just as when, from the conjunction & combining of two fire sticks, heat is generated & fire produced, while from the separation & laying down of those fire sticks the heat coming from them ceases & grows still;
sn48.39:6.2 | S: In the same way, the faculty of pleasure arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as pleasant. | T: in the same way, in dependence on a contact to be experienced as pleasure, the pleasure-faculty arises…
sn48.39:6.3 | S: When in a state of pleasure, you understand: ‘I’m in a state of pleasure.’ | T: (none)
sn48.39:6.4 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as pleasant, you understand that the corresponding faculty of pleasure that arises ceases and stops. | T: (none)
sn48.39:7.1 | S: The faculty of pain … | T: “In dependence on a contact to be experienced as pain, the pain-faculty arises…
sn48.39:7.2 | S: happiness … | T: “In dependence on a contact to be experienced as happiness, the happiness-faculty arises…
sn48.39:7.3 | S: sadness … | T: “In dependence on a contact to be experienced as distress, the distress-faculty arises…
sn48.39:7.4 | S: equanimity arises dependent on a contact to be experienced as equanimous. | T: “In dependence on a contact to be experienced as equanimity, the equanimity-faculty arises.
sn48.39:7.5 | S: When in a state of equanimity, you understand: ‘I’m in a state of equanimity.’ | T: Being equanimous, one discerns, ‘I am equanimous.’
sn48.39:7.6 | S: With the cessation of that contact to be experienced as equanimous, you understand that the corresponding faculty of equanimity that arises ceases and stops.” | T: With the cessation of that very contact to be experienced as equanimity, one discerns, ‘What was experienced as coming from that—the equanimity-faculty arising in dependence on a contact to be experienced as equanimity—ceases & grows still.’”
sn48.39:7.7 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn48.8
sn48.8:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these five faculties. | T: “Monks, there are these five faculties.
sn48.8:1.2 | S: What five? | T: Which five?
sn48.8:1.3 | S: The faculties of faith, energy, mindfulness, immersion, and wisdom. | T: The faculty of conviction, the faculty of persistence, the faculty of mindfulness, the faculty of concentration, & the faculty of discernment.
sn48.8:1.4 | S: And where should the faculty of faith be seen? | T: “Now, where is the faculty of conviction to be seen?
sn48.8:1.5 | S: In the four factors of stream-entry. | T: In the four stream-entry factors:
sn48.8:1.6 | P: ettha saddhindriyaṁ daṭṭhabbaṁ. | T: Here the faculty of conviction is to be seen.
sn48.8:1.7 | S: And where should the faculty of energy be seen? | T: “And where is the faculty of persistence to be seen?
sn48.8:1.8 | S: In the four right efforts. | T: In the four right exertions:
sn48.8:1.9 | P: ettha vīriyindriyaṁ daṭṭhabbaṁ. | T: Here the faculty of persistence is to be seen.
sn48.8:1.10 | S: And where should the faculty of mindfulness be seen? | T: “And where is the faculty of mindfulness to be seen?
sn48.8:1.11 | S: In the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: In the four establishings of mindfulness:
sn48.8:1.12 | P: ettha satindriyaṁ daṭṭhabbaṁ. | T: Here the faculty of mindfulness is to be seen.
sn48.8:1.13 | S: And where should the faculty of immersion be seen? | T: “And where is the faculty of concentration to be seen?
sn48.8:1.14 | S: In the four absorptions. | T: In the four jhānas:
sn48.8:1.15 | P: ettha samādhindriyaṁ daṭṭhabbaṁ. | T: Here the faculty of concentration is to be seen.
sn48.8:1.16 | S: And where should the faculty of wisdom be seen? | T: “And where is the faculty of discernment to be seen?
sn48.8:1.17 | S: In the four noble truths. | T: In the four noble truths:
sn48.8:1.18 | P: ettha paññindriyaṁ daṭṭhabbaṁ. | T: Here the faculty of discernment is to be seen.” See also SN 45:8 and SN 56:11.
sn48.8:1.19 | S: These are the five faculties.” | T: (none)
sn48.8:1.20 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn52.9
sn52.9:1.1 | S: At one time the venerables Anuruddha and Sāriputta were staying near Vesālī in Ambapālī’s Mango Grove. | T: I have heard that on one occasion Ven. Anuruddha & Ven. Sāriputta were staying near Vesālī in Ambapālīs forest.
sn52.9:1.2 | S: Then in the late afternoon, Sāriputta came out of retreat, went to Anuruddha, and said to him: | T: Then Ven. Sāriputta, arising from his seclusion in the late afternoon, went to Ven. Anuruddha. On arrival, he exchanged courteous greetings with him. After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to Ven. Anuruddha,
sn52.9:2.1 | S: “Reverend Anuruddha, your faculties are so very clear, and your complexion is pure and bright. | T: “Bright are your faculties, friend Anuruddha; pure your complexion, and clear.
sn52.9:2.2 | S: What kind of meditation are you usually practicing these days?” | T: By means of what dwelling do you now often dwell?”
sn52.9:2.3 | S: “These days, reverend, I usually meditate with my mind firmly established in the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “I now often dwell, friend, with a mind well-established in the four establishings of mindfulness.
sn52.9:2.4 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
sn52.9:2.5 | S: I meditate observing an aspect of the body … | T: There is the case where I remain focused on the body in & of itself—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
sn52.9:2.6 | S: feelings … | T: I remain focused on feelings in & of themselves… the mind in & of itself…
sn52.9:2.7 | S: mind … | T: (none)
sn52.9:2.8 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: mental qualities in & of themselves—ardent, alert, & mindful—subduing greed & distress with reference to the world.
sn52.9:2.9 | S: These days I usually meditate with my mind firmly established in these four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “I now often dwell, friend, with a mind well-established in these four establishings of mindfulness.
sn52.9:2.10 | S: A mendicant who is perfected—with defilements ended, who has completed the spiritual journey, done what had to be done, laid down the burden, achieved their heart’s goal, utterly ended the fetter of continued existence, and is rightly freed through enlightenment—usually meditates with their mind firmly established in these four kinds of mindfulness meditation.” | T: “Any monk whose effluents are ended—who has reached fulfillment, done the task, laid down the burden, attained the true goal, laid to waste the fetter of becoming, and who is released through right gnosis—often dwells with a mind well-established in these four establishings of mindfulness.”
sn52.9:3.1 | S: “We’re so fortunate, reverend, so very fortunate, | T: “It is a gain for us, my friend, a great gain for us,
sn52.9:3.2 | S: to have heard such a dramatic statement in the presence of Venerable Anuruddha.” | T: that we were right in Ven. Anuruddha’s presence when he said this bull-like statement!”
sn52.9:3.3 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an9.39
an9.39:1.1 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, a battle was fought between the gods and the titans. | T: “Once, monks, a battle between the devas & asuras was in full swing.
an9.39:1.2 | S: In that battle the titans won and the gods lost. | T: And in that battle, the asuras won and the devas lost.
an9.39:1.3 | S: Defeated, the gods fled north with the titans in pursuit. | T: Having lost, the devas simply fled while the asuras, heading north, attacked them.
an9.39:1.4 | S: Then the gods thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to the devas,
an9.39:1.5 | S: ‘The titans are still in pursuit. | T: ‘The asuras are still attacking.
an9.39:1.6 | S: Why don’t we engage them in battle a second time?’ | T: Why don’t we do battle a second time?’
an9.39:1.7 | S: And so a second battle was fought between the gods and the titans. | T: “So the devas did battle with the asuras a second time.
an9.39:1.8 | S: And for a second time the titans won and the gods lost. | T: And a second time, the asuras won and the devas lost.
an9.39:1.9 | S: Defeated, the gods fled north with the titans in pursuit. | T: Having lost, the devas simply fled while the asuras, heading north, attacked them.
an9.39:2.1 | S: Then the gods thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to the devas,
an9.39:2.2 | S: ‘The titans are still in pursuit. | T: ‘The asuras are still attacking.
an9.39:2.3 | S: Why don’t we engage them in battle a third time?’ | T: Why don’t we do battle a third time?’
an9.39:2.4 | S: And so a third battle was fought between the gods and the titans. | T: “So the devas did battle with the asuras a third time.
an9.39:2.5 | S: And for a third time the titans won and the gods lost. | T: And a third time, the asuras won and the devas lost.
an9.39:2.6 | S: Defeated and terrified, the gods fled right into the castle of the gods. | T: Having lost, the devas, afraid, entered the deva capital.
an9.39:2.7 | S: When they had entered their castle, they thought, | T: Having gone to their capital, the devas thought,
an9.39:2.8 | S: ‘Now we’re in a secure location and the titans can’t do anything to us.’ | T: ‘Having come to this shelter for the fearful, we will now keep here to ourselves, having nothing to do with the asuras.’
an9.39:2.9 | S: The titans also thought, | T: And the asuras also thought,
an9.39:2.10 | S: ‘Now the gods are in a secure location and we can’t do anything to them.’ | T: ‘Having gone to this shelter for the fearful, the devas will now keep here to themselves, having nothing to do with us.’
an9.39:3.1 | S: Once upon a time, a battle was fought between the gods and the titans. | T: “Once, monks, a battle between the devas & asuras was in full swing.
an9.39:3.2 | S: In that battle the gods won and the titans lost. | T: And in that battle, the devas won and the asuras lost.
an9.39:3.3 | S: Defeated, the titans fled south with the gods in pursuit. | T: Having lost, the asuras simply fled while the devas, heading south, attacked them.
an9.39:3.4 | S: Then the titans thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to the asura,
an9.39:3.5 | S: ‘The gods are still in pursuit. | T: ‘The devas are still attacking.
an9.39:3.6 | S: Why don’t we engage them in battle a second time?’ | T: Why don’t we do battle a second time?’
an9.39:3.7 | S: And so a second battle was fought between the gods and the titans. | T: “So the asuras did battle with the devas a second time.
an9.39:3.8 | S: And for a second time the gods won and the titans lost. | T: And a second time, the devas won and the asuras lost.
an9.39:3.9 | S: Defeated, the titans fled south with the gods in pursuit. | T: Having lost, the asuras simply fled while the devas, heading south, attacked them.
an9.39:4.1 | S: Then the titans thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to the asuras,
an9.39:4.2 | S: ‘The gods are still in pursuit. | T: ‘The devas are still attacking.
an9.39:4.3 | S: Why don’t we engage them in battle a third time?’ | T: Why don’t we do battle a third time?’
an9.39:4.4 | S: And so a third battle was fought between the gods and the titans. | T: “So the asuras did battle with the devas a third time.
an9.39:4.5 | S: And for a third time the gods won and the titans lost. | T: And a third time, the devas won and the asuras lost.
an9.39:4.6 | S: Defeated and terrified, the titans fled right into the citadel of the titans. | T: Having lost, the asuras, afraid, entered the asura capital.
an9.39:4.7 | S: When they had entered their citadel, they thought, | T: Having gone to their capital, the asuras thought,
an9.39:4.8 | S: ‘Now we’re in a secure location and the gods can’t do anything to us.’ | T: ‘Having come to this shelter for the fearful, we will now keep here to ourselves, having nothing to do with the devas.’
an9.39:4.9 | S: And the gods also thought, | T: And the devas also thought,
an9.39:4.10 | S: ‘Now the titans are in a secure location and we can’t do anything to them.’ | T: ‘Having gone to this shelter for the fearful, the asuras will now keep here to themselves, having nothing to do with us.’
an9.39:5.1 | S: In the same way, there’s a time when a mendicant, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: “In the same way, monks, on whatever occasion a monk—secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities—enters the first jhāna: rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation,
an9.39:5.2 | S: At that time the mendicant thinks, | T: on that occasion the thought occurs to him,
an9.39:5.3 | S: ‘Now I’m in a secure location and Māra can’t do anything to me.’ | T: ‘Having come to this shelter for the fearful, I will now keep here to myself, having nothing to do with Māra.’
an9.39:5.4 | S: And Māra the Wicked also thinks, | T: And the thought occurs to Māra, the Evil One,
an9.39:5.5 | S: ‘Now the mendicant is in a secure location and we can’t do anything to them.’ | T: ‘Having gone to this shelter for the fearful, the monk will now keep here to himself, having nothing to do with me.’
an9.39:6.1 | S: There’s a time when, as the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, a mendicant enters and remains in the second absorption … | T: [Similarly with the second,
an9.39:6.2 | S: third absorption … | T: third,
an9.39:6.3 | S: fourth absorption. | T: & fourth jhānas.]
an9.39:6.4 | S: At that time the mendicant thinks, | T: (none)
an9.39:6.5 | S: ‘Now I’m in a secure location and Māra can’t do anything to me.’ | T: (none)
an9.39:6.6 | S: And Māra the Wicked also thinks, | T: (none)
an9.39:6.7 | S: ‘Now the mendicant is in a secure location and we can’t do anything to them.’ | T: (none)
an9.39:7.1 | S: There’s a time when a mendicant, going totally beyond perceptions of form, with the disappearance of perceptions of impingement, not focusing on perceptions of diversity, aware that ‘space is infinite’, enters and remains in the dimension of infinite space. | T: “On whatever occasion a monk, with the complete transcending of perceptions of (physical) form, with the disappearance of perceptions of resistance, and not attending to perceptions of multiplicity, (perceiving,) ‘Infinite space,’ enters the dimension of the infinitude of space:
an9.39:7.2 | S: This is called a mendicant who has blinded Māra, put out his eyes without a trace, and gone where the Wicked One cannot see. | T: He is said to be a monk who has put Māra in the dark. Having bound Māra’s eyes and leaving no trace, he has become invisible to the Evil One.
an9.39:8.1 | S: There’s a time when a mendicant, going totally beyond the dimension of infinite space, aware that ‘consciousness is infinite’, enters and remains in the dimension of infinite consciousness. … | T: [Similarly with the dimensions of the infinitude of consciousness,
an9.39:8.2 | S: Going totally beyond the dimension of infinite consciousness, aware that ‘there is nothing at all’, they enter and remain in the dimension of nothingness. … | T: nothingness,
an9.39:8.3 | S: Going totally beyond the dimension of nothingness, they enter and remain in the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception. … | T: and neither perception nor non-perception.]
an9.39:8.4 | S: Going totally beyond the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, they enter and remain in the cessation of perception and feeling. And, having seen with wisdom, their defilements come to an end. | T: “On whatever occasion, with the complete transcending of the dimension of neither perception nor non-perception, he enters & remains in the cessation of perception & feeling; and as he sees (that) with discernment, effluents are completely ended:
an9.39:8.5 | S: This is called a mendicant who has blinded Māra, put out his eyes without a trace, and gone where the Wicked One cannot see. And they’ve crossed over clinging to the world.” | T: He is said to be a monk who has put Māra in the dark. Having bound Māra’s eyes and leaving no trace, he has become invisible to the Evil One, having crossed over attachment in the cosmos.”
an9.39:8.6 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an7.50
an7.50:1.1 | S: Then the brahmin Jānussoṇi went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Jāṇussoṇin the brahman went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an7.50:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha, | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to the Blessed One,
an7.50:1.3 | S: “Does Worthy Gotama claim to be chaste?” | T: “Does Master Gotama claim to be one who leads the holy life?”
an7.50:1.4 | S: “Brahmin, if anyone should be rightly said to | T: “If, brahman, one could rightly say of anyone,
an7.50:1.5 | S: live the chaste life intact, impeccable, spotless, and unmarred, full and pure, it’s me.” | T: ‘He leads the holy life without gap, without break, without spot, without blemish—perfect & pure,’ it would rightly be said of me.
an7.50:1.6 | P: ‘ahañhi, brāhmaṇa, akhaṇḍaṁ acchiddaṁ asabalaṁ akammāsaṁ paripuṇṇaṁ parisuddhaṁ brahmacariyaṁ carāmī’”ti. | T: I lead the holy life without gap, without break, without spot, without blemish—perfect & pure.”
an7.50:1.7 | S: “But what, Worthy Gotama, is a break, taint, stain, or mar in chastity?” | T: “But what, Master Gotama, is a gap, a break, a spot, a blemish of the holy life?”
an7.50:2.1 | S: “Firstly, an ascetic or brahmin who claims to be perfectly chaste does not mutually engage in sex with a lady. | T: “There is the case, brahman, where a certain contemplative or brahman, while claiming to be one who rightly follows the holy life, doesn’t actually engage in copulating with a woman
an7.50:2.2 | S: However, they consent to being anointed, massaged, bathed, and rubbed by a lady. | T: but he does consent to being anointed, rubbed down, bathed, or massaged by a woman.
an7.50:2.3 | S: They enjoy it and like it and find it rewarding. | T: He enjoys that, wants more of that, and luxuriates in that.
an7.50:2.4 | S: This is a break, taint, stain, or mar in chastity. | T: This is a gap, a break, a spot, a blemish of the holy life.
an7.50:2.5 | S: This is called one who lives the chaste life impurely, yoked by the yoke of sex. They’re not freed from rebirth, old age, death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, sadness, and distress. They’re not freed from suffering, I say. | T: He is called one who lives the holy life in an impure way, one who is fettered by the fetter of sexuality. He is not freed from birth, aging, & death, from sorrows, lamentations, pains, griefs, & despairs. He is not freed, I tell you, from suffering & stress.
an7.50:3.1 | S: Furthermore, an ascetic or brahmin who claims to be perfectly chaste does not mutually engage in sex with a lady. Nor do they consent to massage and bathing. | T: (none)

