an7.50:9.1 | S: As long as I saw that these seven sexual yokes—or even one of them—had not been given up in me, I didn’t announce my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. | T: “And, brahman, as long as I saw that one or another of these seven fetters of sexuality was not abandoned in myself, I did not claim to have directly awakened to the right self-awakening unexcelled in the cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, their royalty & commonfolk.
an7.50:10.1 | S: But when I saw that these seven sexual yokes—every one of them—had been given up in me, I announced my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. | T: But when I did not see any one of these seven fetters of sexuality unabandoned in myself, then I did claim to have directly awakened to the right self-awakening unexcelled in the cosmos with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, their royalty & commonfolk.
an7.50:10.2 | S: Knowledge and vision arose in me: | T: Knowledge & vision arose in me:
an7.50:10.3 | S: ‘My freedom is unshakable; this is my last rebirth; now there’ll be no more future lives.’” | T: ‘Unprovoked is my release. This is the last birth. There is now no further becoming.’”
an7.50:11.1 | S: When he said this, the brahmin Jānussoṇi said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Jāṇussoṇin the brahman said to the Blessed One,
an7.50:11.2 | S: “Excellent, worthy Gotama! Excellent! … | T: “Magnificent, Master Gotama! Magnificent! Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has Master Gotama—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear. I go to Master Gotama for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks.
an7.50:11.3 | S: From this day forth, may the worthy Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: May Master Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge from this day forward, for life.”
an7.50:11.4 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn48.53
sn48.53:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn48.53:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Kosambī in Ghosita’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Kosambī at Ghosita’s monastery.
sn48.53:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There he addressed the monks,
sn48.53:1.4 | S: “Mendicants, is there a way that a mendicant who is a trainee, standing at the level of a trainee, can understand that they are a trainee? And that a mendicant who is an adept, standing at the level of an adept, can understand that they are an adept?” | T: “Monks, is there a manner of reckoning whereby a monk who is a learner [i.e., a person who has attained at least stream-entry, but has not yet reached arahantship], standing at the level of a learner, can discern that ‘I am a learner,’ and whereby a monk who is an adept [i.e., an arahant], standing at the level of an adept, can discern that ‘I am an adept’?”
sn48.53:2.1 | S: “Our teachings are rooted in the Buddha. …” | T: “For us, lord, the teachings have the Blessed One as their root, their guide, & their arbitrator. It would be good if the Blessed One himself would explicate the meaning of this statement. Having heard it from the Blessed One, the monks will remember it.” “In that case, monks, listen & pay close attention. I will speak.” “As you say, lord,” the monks responded. The Blessed One said,
sn48.53:2.2 | S: “There is a way that a mendicant who is a trainee, standing at the level of a trainee, can understand that they are a trainee, and that a mendicant who is an adept, standing at the level of an adept, can understand that they are an adept. | T: “There is a manner of reckoning whereby a monk who is a learner, standing at the level of a learner, can discern that ‘I am a learner,’ and whereby a monk who is an adept, standing at the level of an adept, can discern that ‘I am an adept.’
sn48.53:3.1 | S: And what is a way that a mendicant who is a trainee can understand that they are a trainee? | T: “And what is the manner of reckoning whereby a monk who is a learner, standing at the level of a learner, can discern that ‘I am a learner’?
sn48.53:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant who is a trainee truly understands: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: There is the case where a monk is a learner. He discerns, as it has come to be, that ‘This is stress… This is the origination of stress… This is the cessation of stress… This is the path of practice leading to the cessation of stress.’
sn48.53:3.3 | S: This is a way that a mendicant who is a trainee can understand that they are a trainee. | T: This is a manner of reckoning whereby a monk who is a learner, standing at the level of a learner, can discern that ‘I am a learner.’
sn48.53:4.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant who is a trainee reflects: | T: “And further, the monk who is a learner reflects,
sn48.53:4.2 | S: ‘Is there any other ascetic or brahmin elsewhere whose teaching is as true, as real, as accurate as that of the Buddha?’ | T: ‘Is there outside of this (Dhamma & Vinaya) any contemplative or brahman who teaches the true, genuine, & accurate Dhamma like the Blessed One?’
sn48.53:4.3 | S: They understand: | T: And he discerns,
sn48.53:4.4 | S: ‘There is no other ascetic or brahmin elsewhere whose teaching is as true, as real, as accurate as that of the Buddha.’ | T: ‘No, there is no contemplative or brahman outside of this who teaches the true, genuine, & accurate Dhamma like the Blessed One.’
sn48.53:4.5 | S: This too is a way that a mendicant who is a trainee can understand that they are a trainee. | T: This too is a manner of reckoning whereby a monk who is a learner, standing at the level of a learner, can discern that ‘I am a learner.’
sn48.53:5.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant who is a trainee understands the five faculties: | T: “And further, the monk who is a learner discerns the five faculties:
sn48.53:5.2 | S: faith, energy, mindfulness, immersion, and wisdom. | T: the faculty of conviction… persistence… mindfulness… concentration… discernment.
sn48.53:5.3 | S: And although they don’t have direct meditative experience of their destination, apex, fruit, and culmination, | T: He sees clear through with discernment their destiny, excellence, rewards, & consummation, but he does not touch them with his body.
sn48.53:5.4 | S: they do see them with penetrating wisdom. | T: (none)
sn48.53:5.5 | S: This too is a way that a mendicant who is a trainee can understand that they are a trainee. | T: This too is a manner of reckoning whereby a monk who is a learner, standing at the level of a learner, can discern that ‘I am a learner.’
sn48.53:6.1 | S: And what is the way that a mendicant who is an adept can understand that they are an adept? | T: “And what is the manner of reckoning whereby a monk who is an adept, standing at the level of an adept, can discern that ‘I am an adept’?
sn48.53:6.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant who is an adept understands the five faculties: | T: There is the case where a monk who is an adept discerns the five faculties:
sn48.53:6.3 | S: faith, energy, mindfulness, immersion, and wisdom. | T: the faculty of conviction… persistence… mindfulness… concentration… discernment.
sn48.53:6.4 | S: They have direct meditative experience of their destination, apex, fruit, and culmination, | T: He touches with his body and sees clear through with discernment what their destiny, excellence, rewards, & consummation are.
sn48.53:6.5 | S: and they see them with penetrating wisdom. | T: (none)
sn48.53:6.6 | S: This is a way that a mendicant who is an adept can understand that they are an adept. | T: This is a manner of reckoning whereby a monk who is an adept, standing at the level of an adept, can discern that ‘I am an adept.’
sn48.53:7.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant who is an adept understands the six faculties: | T: “And further, the monk who is an adept discerns the six sense faculties:
sn48.53:7.2 | S: eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind. | T: the faculty of the eye… ear… nose…tongue… body… intellect.
sn48.53:7.3 | S: They understand: ‘These six faculties will totally and utterly cease without remainder. And no other six faculties will arise anywhere anyhow.’ | T: He discerns, ‘These six sense faculties will disband entirely, everywhere, & in every way without remainder, and no other set of six sense faculties will arise anywhere or in any way.’
sn48.53:7.4 | S: This too is a way that a mendicant who is an adept can understand that they are an adept.” | T: This too is a manner of reckoning whereby a monk who is an adept, standing at the level of an adept, can discern that ‘I am an adept.’”
sn48.53:7.5 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)


